import { DocsPage, Demo, Code, ApiReference } from "../../shell.rx"
import { CalendarHero } from "../../demos/calendar/hero.rx"
import { CalendarRange } from "../../demos/calendar/range.rx"
import { CalendarConstraints } from "../../demos/calendar/constraints.rx"
import { CalendarSelects } from "../../demos/calendar/selects.rx"
import { CalendarPicker } from "../../demos/calendar/picker.rx"

export default component Page() {
  <DocsPage title="Calendar" subtitle="A month grid for choosing a day, or a range of days.">
    <Demo source="calendar/hero"><CalendarHero /></Demo>

    <h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>
    <p>
      Import the parts and put them together. <code>CalendarRoot</code> chooses a day and{" "}
      <code>RangeCalendarRoot</code> a range; every other part works inside either.
    </p>
    <Code file="calendar/anatomy.rx.txt" />

    <h2 id="platform">What the browser does</h2>
    <p>
      <code>{"<input type=\"date\">"}</code> is supported in every browser, but its picker belongs to the browser.
      It can't be styled, shown inline on the page, shown as several months, or used to choose a range. So it can't
      be the calendar. The calendar is built from the elements that do fit:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Each month is a real <code>{"<table role=\"grid\">"}</code>. Rows, column headers and cells come from the
        table itself, and the weekday headers are <code>{"<th scope=\"col\">"}</code> with the long name of the day.
        Divs would need a role on every row and cell to say the same.
      </li>
      <li>
        Each day is a native <code>{"<button>"}</code>. <kbd>Enter</kbd> and <kbd>Space</kbd> press it,{" "}
        <code>disabled</code> takes it out of the focus order, and <code>:focus-visible</code> styles the focused
        day.
      </li>
      <li>The month and year pickers are native <code>{"<select>"}</code> elements, with the browser's list and keyboard.</li>
      <li>
        Dates are <code>Temporal.PlainDate</code> values. Safari doesn't ship Temporal yet, so an app loads a polyfill
        until it does. The library never imports one.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The library adds the grid's keyboard, which the platform doesn't have. It also adds a single Tab stop,
      selection, range preview, <code>min</code> and <code>max</code>, unavailable days, several months side by side,
      and the form and <a href="../field/">Field</a> wiring.
    </p>
    <div class="note">
      <p>
        There are no Month, DayGridRow or DayGridHeaderRow parts. Rows and header rows carry no behaviour, so write
        a plain <code>{"<tr>"}</code>, or let <code>CalendarGridHeader</code> and <code>CalendarGridBody</code>{" "}
        render them. A grid's month comes from its <code>offset</code> among the visible months.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="examples">Examples</h2>

    <h3 id="keyboard">Keyboard</h3>
    <p>
      The grid is one Tab stop. That stop is the day focus was last on, or else the selected day, then today, then
      the first day that can be chosen. Inside the grid the keys follow the WAI-ARIA date picker pattern:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>The arrow keys move by a day and a week. In a right-to-left layout, left and right are swapped.</li>
      <li><kbd>Home</kbd> and <kbd>End</kbd> go to the start and end of the week.</li>
      <li><kbd>PageUp</kbd> and <kbd>PageDown</kbd> move by a month; with <kbd>Shift</kbd>, by a year.</li>
      <li><kbd>Enter</kbd> and <kbd>Space</kbd> choose the focused day.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      If focus moves past the visible months, the page turns and focus stays on the new day. The month label is a
      polite live region (<code>aria-live="polite"</code>), for the new month to be read out. The first day of the week and the weekend days come from
      the <code>locale</code> (<code>firstDayOfWeek</code> overrides the first day), and the days are named in that
      locale.
    </p>

    <h3 id="range">Choosing a range</h3>
    <p>
      In a <code>RangeCalendarRoot</code>, the first press sets the start of the range. Moving the pointer or
      focus previews the range, and a second press completes it, in either order. <kbd>Escape</kbd> cancels a
      range that is under way. Days carry <code>data-range-start</code>, <code>data-range-middle</code> and{" "}
      <code>data-range-end</code>, and <code>data-range-preview</code> while the second day is still being chosen.
      Pass <code>numberOfMonths</code> to show several months, which turn a page at a time. Render them from the
      root's <code>months</code>, as this demo does.
    </p>
    <Demo source="calendar/range"><CalendarRange /></Demo>
    <p>
      A range can't include an unavailable day unless you pass <code>allowsNonContiguousRanges</code>. The preview
      stops at its first day, and a second press past an unavailable day is ignored.
    </p>

    <h3 id="constraints">Limits and unavailable days</h3>
    <p>
      Days before <code>min</code> or after <code>max</code> are disabled buttons. They can't be focused, and the
      previous and next buttons stop at the limits. Days for which <code>isDateUnavailable</code> returns true stay
      focusable, so the keyboard still reaches them. They carry{" "}
      <code>aria-disabled="true"</code> and <code>data-unavailable</code>, and can't be chosen.{" "}
      <code>showWeekNumbers</code> adds a column of ISO week numbers.
    </p>
    <Demo source="calendar/constraints"><CalendarConstraints /></Demo>

    <h3 id="selects">Month and year selects</h3>
    <p>
      <code>CalendarMonthSelect</code> and <code>CalendarYearSelect</code> are native selects that set the visible
      month. A month or year with no day between <code>min</code> and <code>max</code> is a disabled option.{" "}
      <code>fixedWeeks</code> always shows six rows, so the grid keeps its height from month to month.
    </p>
    <Demo source="calendar/selects"><CalendarSelects /></Demo>

    <h3 id="picker">In a popover</h3>
    <p>
      A date picker is a calendar in a <a href="../popover/">Popover</a>, with a controlled <code>value</code>.{" "}
      <code>autoFocus={"{"}open{"}"}</code> moves focus to the chosen day (a range's start) when the popup opens,
      turning to its month, and drops a range left half-chosen; with nothing chosen, focus goes to today.{" "}
      <code>onSelectionComplete</code> is called when a choice is finished, so the popup can close. For a single
      day that is every press, even on the day already chosen; for a range it is the second press. While a range is
      under way, the first <kbd>Escape</kbd> cancels it and leaves the popup open.
    </p>
    <Demo source="calendar/picker"><CalendarPicker /></Demo>
    <p>
      Otherwise the visible month doesn't follow a controlled <code>value</code>. Pass <code>month</code> to choose
      it. A part inside the root can also read <code>use calendar</code> and call <code>focusDate(date)</code> to
      show and focus a day.
    </p>

    <h3 id="forms">Forms and fields</h3>
    <p>
      With a <code>name</code>, <code>CalendarRoot</code> submits the chosen day as an ISO date (
      <code>2026-11-17</code>), or an empty string when there is none. <code>RangeCalendarRoot</code> submits two
      entries, <code>{"<name>-start"}</code> and <code>{"<name>-end"}</code>, unless <code>startName</code> and{" "}
      <code>endName</code> name them. A form reset puts the default value back. Inside a{" "}
      <code>FieldRoot</code>, the field's label names the calendar, and <code>required</code> is validated with the
      field. Pass <code>value={"{"}null{"}"}</code> for a controlled calendar with nothing chosen.
    </p>
    <p>
      The calendar calls <code>onValueChange</code> in a change of its own, so a handler that saves to a server
      never holds the calendar. Write the handler with a <code>guess</code> and the new day shows at once.
    </p>

    <h3 id="custom">Rendering the days yourself</h3>
    <p>
      <code>CalendarGridBody</code> renders a row per week and a <code>CalendarDayCell</code> with a{" "}
      <code>CalendarDay</code> per day. To render them yourself, read <code>use calendarGridState</code> inside a{" "}
      <code>CalendarGrid</code>. Loop over its <code>weeks</code> and each week's <code>days</code> with keyed
      equations (<code>weeks key key = g.weeks</code>), as the anatomy shows, so each day keeps its element when
      focus moves. A <code>CalendarDay</code> with children shows them in place of the day's number. A custom part
      can read the day's state with <code>use calendarCell</code>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="api-reference">API reference</h2>
    <ApiReference family="calendar" />
  </DocsPage>
}
